Send Annex-B H.264 frames and decode them with WebCodecs.

The fMP4 muxer existed only to feed MediaSource. A media element keeps a reserve
of frames so that playback stays smooth, and that reserve was the largest single
delay in the system. libavformat is removed entirely: each encoded packet is
handed to Rust behind a nine-byte header carrying a keyframe flag and the
capture timestamp, and the page feeds the frames to a VideoDecoder and paints
them on a canvas. The codec string is read out of the stream's own SPS
rather than hardcoded, so a hardware encoder that picks a different profile
still decodes.

AV_CODEC_FLAG_GLOBAL_HEADER must stay unset: it moves SPS/PPS into extradata,
and there is no container left to carry them. Unset, x264 repeats them in-band
ahead of every IDR, which is what the decoder needs to configure itself.

gop_size goes from 12 to 120 in the same change. Keyframes bought nothing once
the client stopped seeking to the live edge on every frame, and each one is a
bandwidth spike; 120 is as far as it can go before a joining client waits too
long for its first picture.

write_video_packet also gains extern "C". #[no_mangle] on a plain fn is
formally undefined behaviour.

